    "turn_idx": 282,
    "turn_text": "It really was when I got married, we sort of started on that journey. We got married and neither of us were, I would say, particularly strong Catholics at the time. She probably was in better shape than I was in many respects. But we got married and You know, when you get married, you sort of sit back and particularly we got pregnant within like three or four months after we got married and all of a sudden now you got, hey, you got a kid coming and it's sort of time to grow up. Now I was running for Congress this time but still it was from a standpoint of your own personal behavior and your personal morals and ethics. It's just time to, now you've got to set an example. Now you've got to form the next generation and you've got You have that responsibility that to me was very heavy for both of Karen and me. And so we decided to get more engaged and involved in our faith community and not just the church itself, but outside the church and reading. And so we went on this journey for a little bit, but it really wasn't until I came to the United States Senate. because my first four years of Congress, I mean, I was just, I mean, I was running. And then, you know, I got elected and I knew I was going to be in this new district that was a horrible district that no one gave me a chance. So I just, I was just a maniac. I mean, I just went everywhere, did everything. And we had two little babies at the time. And then, um, so I won reelection decided shortly after that, that I probably wouldn't survive in that district for very long. It would take one bad year, and no matter what my favor—and by the way, I got 63 percent, a 70 percent registered democratic district. But I knew that over time, votes would catch up with you, because I was pretty conservative. I thought I could move the district. And as you see, now Western Pennsylvania has moved dramatically. And a lot of those voters now vote continually Republican. And I was probably the first one they voted for. But now I'm probably the norm for what they vote for. But I decided to take a crack at a Senate race. And that you run for the Senate and you're in the House. I mean, I was a maniac.",
