    "turn_text": "risk to your wife's? Well, the surgery itself was not, well, I guess ultimately it did have a risk to her, but they had intrauterine surgery up in Philadelphia and at the University of Pennsylvania Hospital. And the surgery actually worked. It was experimental, but it worked. But she got an infection. Because of the surgery, it opens the womb up to infection. And the way the body deals with an infection in the amniotic sac, which is where the baby is, is to expel the infection. And so she wanted the labor. And we knew when she had a high temperature three days later, We were very happy for three days and she got a high temperature, started going to labor and the threat to her life at that point is had she not delivered that baby, she and the baby both would have died. And so she ended up delivering the baby and he was born alive and lived two hours with us and we held him and loved him as long as we could.",
