    "turn_idx": 329,
    "turn_text": "On the contraception side, the position I've always said, my wife and I don't do that, and because we want it to be open for whatever God has planned for us, and we'll accept that, and we'll accept the responsibility, in many cases, the burden that comes with that. And we just feel like that the church's teaching is right on this, that contraception separates the act of love to the the end of that, which is for having children, not to say that you can't have sexual relations without having children, but that's the church's teaching is that they should always be combined, and that's the best way to do it, and it's not just pleasure for pleasure's sake. And so we accept that teaching, and so we've lived by it. And the interesting is that, at no point in time in my political career, I've ever tried to impose that on any piece of legislation or any public policy at all. But I was criticized fairly heavily for having that point of view from a personal point of",
